s  you know if I try to limit myself to 90%  known words I would take forever to get  anywhere and if you are in an  environment such as link where you have  access to online dictionaries where you  have the audio you can click on words  and see the meaning right away you can  click on words or phrases see the  meaning here then pronounce granted  text-to-speech then you didn't listen  the natural audio again you can take it  away and listen several times go back  and read again that this in this  environment you can actually tackle more  difficult material and progress more  quickly if you you know take on a higher  percentage of unknown words now you have  to be motivated you have to be willing  to deal with the uncertainty of having  maybe 20% of the words there that you  don't understand the first time through  you have to be willing to go back to it  again all of those things are true and  maybe that doesn't work in a classroom  but so that there's there's the first  thing I think you know we should  challenge students in my opinion maybe  even in the classroom to take on content  that's a little more difficult than just  2% unknown words the second thing is you  know start with stories sure a lot of  these stories that they use are sort  fantasy fairy tale folktale imagination  as an adult I don't find these stories  very compelling or very interesting I  prefer for example the stories that we  have a link which are daily John works  in a restaurant he's a cook he drives to  work he drives home simple stories that  are relatively meaningless but embody  the most common verbs in the language  they do have some meaning and the  stories repeat the vocabulary over and  over again so to the extent that I'm  motivated to learn the language these  stories in fact are in a way compelling  one other thing is you know I mentioned  in a tweet that I think noticing is a  big issue in language learning because  when we begin learning we just it's just  noise we don't notice anything we don't  notice where one word ends the next word  begins we don't notice the functions of  the words nothing and in time as we  start to save words and start to  understand which words are verbs in  which words are nouns and what they mean  then we start to notice other things we  start to notice connections between  words we start to notice the  pronunciation and so my question is as  an independent learner you know how can  i how can i improve my ability to notice  because when i notice more i learn more  now the minute i said that in a tweet i  get involved in the sort of disputes  that go on in the sort of language  acquisition field where someone has done  noticing hypothesis and there's an  output hypothesis and everyone disagrees  with each other and it's as if the  person who did this noticing hypothesis  and tied this to some kind of deliberate  study of grammar that that has forever  defined the meaning of notice okay my  meaning of notice is different i was  doing the dishes earlier here cleaning  up after breakfast and i'm listening to  our many stories in arabic for the i'm  try don't know 30 or 40 of time and  every time i listen i noticed something  that i hadn't noticed before i'm aware  of myself noticing i'm not deliberately  noticing but i'm  where that I notice an ending or in the  case of Arabic verbs the beginning of  the verb I noticed a certain word that I  hadn't noticed before our certain phrase  I shouldn't say that I hadn't noticed  before I might have noticed and then  forgotten and noticed again and the same  when I review the lessons on my iPad  I've gone through the lesson I've saved  all of you know I've changed all the  blue words that I needed to yell all the  unknown words have been saved so I  review it now before I start reviewing  the lesson the story I go through that  might be 20 yellow links that are in  that lesson I review them in my  flashcards and then I listen to the  story while reading it and I find that 